sue of alliances  with the benefit of years of advanced  education he earned master's degrees in  philosophy politics and economics as a  Rhodes Scholar and attended the advanced  management program at the Harvard  Business School will you welcome senator  died and mr Turner thank you very very  much let me at the outset express my  gratitude to the Admiral for allowing me  to go first we're in the process as your  gathered here of voting on the floor of  the Senate in fact on a war powers  resolution dealing with the Persian Gulf  so the topic is timely and and I'm  fearful only that those bells may go off  and I'll be called back to the floor of  the Senate if a vote occurs in the next  few minutes so I'll try and keep my  remarks relatively brief to make the  points I'd like to and then listen to  the Admiral and of time permits  obviously to be around to respond any  questions that you may have first of all  I thank you for that very generous and  kind introduction and let me commend all  of you for being here than these days  particularly must be frustrating I  suppose with all of the action in New  York at these particular moments with  the watching the market and the like but  nonetheless I think most would agree to  some extent foreign policy events  obviously have an impact on market  fluctuations whether or not you can  attribute the events the Persian Gulf to  being a major cause or a minor cause or  any cause at all is open for debate and  discussion but nonetheless certainly as  we live in a ever shrinking world events  which in the past might have taken days  to reach us now we can virtually watch  as they occur I suspect in fact we have  seen instances where military  engagements could be filmed live it's  not uncommon for Israeli pilots during  the brute lebanon conflict to literally  be home by the evening news and watch  themselves in forays over lebanon and  certainly if we know in the last 24 48  hours almost as the events occurred in  the Persian Gulf the secretary defense  was reporting them to the American  people so clearly today we live in a far  more complex a world where information  is readily available to all  instantaneously in an effect has an  impact on decisions I suspect that  things might not have been quite as bad  yesterday in the market were there not  constant information being pumped out  across this country about what was  occurring I suspect that contributed  significantly to the Stampede mentality  that caused the unprecedented action and  the market yesterday but the best  example I guess I can think of to  discuss the question of us commitments  as we close out this century is the  Persian Gulf issue and the debate  surrounding the War Powers Resolution  let me express a couple of comments on  that first of all at a time when most of  international armed conflicts started  with a formal declaration of war our  Constitution assigned as you all know a  right to declare wolf a war solely to  the Congress of the United States during  the 20th century the and especially  after World War two of course the formal  declaration of war gradually disappeared  from the practices of States not only  our own but others around the globe in  the absence of such declaration our  presidents felt increasingly free to  take our armed forces into international  conflicts without the formal consent of  Congress perhaps of course the most  clear example of that presidential  decisions involving the commitment of US  forces was the one fought in Vietnam  while it is true that Congress provided  the money for that conflict and  I would argue strongly escape the  responsibility Congress as you all know  never had a chance if you will or the  opportunity to formally approve or  disapprove the sending of troops to  Vietnam it was dragged along in a way  that was constitutionally ambiguous this  ambiguity and the resulting sense of the  legitimacy substantially I believe  contributed to the national division  resulting from that war that caused such  a tremendous damage to our own self  confidence in the cohesion of our nation  following the end of the Vietnam War  Congress passed the War Powers Act to  restore the original intent or at least  the effort was to restore the original  intent of the Constitution and to be  cognizant of the changing world and the  changing nature of conflict and to  provide for the executive legislative  cooperation without which our nation  would not be able to defend of course  its interests effectively the War Powers  Act is not I would argue an intrusion on  presidential powers it was meant to be a  guarantee that Congress's constitutional  role roll Mayo a role in war making  would be restored over 40 long years we  have had a naval presence in the Persian  Gulf and that is not in dispute our  peaceful presence patrolling the sea  lanes was never questioned by the  Congress and I went to Delhi today it is  not question today either if there were  to be a vote this afternoon on whether  or not the United States ought to be  militarily committed to the Persian Gulf  I would suspect that such a resolution  would pass overwhelmingly in the United  States Senate and I suspect with an  extremely strong vote and the house of  representatives as well unfortunately  there's been confusion over these past  several weeks because of the debate  about the War Powers Resolution and  whether or not it ought to be invoked I  think it's regrettable that we have  ended up bogged down in that debate  because I think it has sent a very  confusing signal not only to our allies  but more importantly to our adversaries  at this particular juncture about  something that is vitally important if a  message is to be heard as effectively as  it could be and that is what I suspect  is the broad-based support across this  country of ours  for the decision to place military  forces in the Gulf to protect and keep  those sea lanes open and free now there  would be I would argue quickly a debate  over whether or not the fleet reef  lagging issue made a great deal of sense  in fact many would look back I think  today and those who supported it and  questioned whether or not that was a  wise decision there were many other ways  of achieving the same result without  going through that gymnastic effort and  maneuver in order to protect those  vessels moving through but on the bottom  line question of whether or not the  United States has an obligation to  commit itself to the free transit of  resources in the Persian Gulf on that  issue that important issue that I think  you would find as I said a moment ago  resounding support now several armed  incidents have taken place since the  stark incident are shooting in capturing  an Iranian mine Lang ship on September  21st the sinking of three gunboats on  october eight and yesterday's  retaliation for the missile attack on  one of the reflagged tankers makes it  abundantly clear i believe that we are  in an armed conflict and the President  cannot legally avoid I happen to believe  compliance with the War Powers Act  anymore for the time being this would  involve the sending of a report to  Congress after which the president would  have 60 to 90 days before Congress could  buy action or inaction mandate the  withdrawal of those forces I frankly do  not understand why the president and his  advisors dread so much the invocation of  the War Powers Act while I and many  members of Congress have had misgivings  as I said about the wisdom of the reef  lagging effort I'm also convinced that  if we had to vote today of course our  presence and the Gulf and even the  continuation of the escort operation  would be confirmed by the Congress I  fail to see how the president imagines  continuing an international conflict for  an extended period without achieving  consensus with the Congress makes sense  it seems to me that he can only be  strengthened by getting Congress's  approval of our military operations our  historic experience shows that Congress  is an extremely reluctant or extremely  rather  reluctant to interfere with presidents  while an armed conflict is impending or  continuing in fact what is pending  before the Senate at this moment under  the condition of cloture is the bird  Warner Amendment which is an alternative  to the straight invocation of the War  Powers Act which considerably eases  those conditions these are the major  differences one we would provide under  bird Warner which passed by the way  longer deadlines than the War Powers  Resolution the substitute targets the  reef lagging escorting operation and not  directly the presence of our forces and  thirdly the substitute requires  congressional action our forces  withdrawal cannot be mandated by  inaction by the Congress that  substantially changes the War Powers  Resolution but nonetheless we think  would involve the Congress at a time  when that kind of involvement could only  be seen or should be seen in a  constructive manner let me emphasize if  I can one of the points I tried to make  here again I think yesterday the  decision to hit targets that were not  armed and caused no loss of life  apparently was the proper response for  the president he could have chosen I  think would have been supported had he  chosen a target of more substance and  more significance but he made the  decision to choose a target that would  send the message without escalating  necessarily the response that we may end  up having from the Iranians but  nonetheless I think it was an  appropriate and proper response where we  do have today that Congress as I believe  we will before the day is out coinciding  agreeing at least on the bottom line  issue the fundamental question I would  think that would also have a profound  impact on Iran and others who might  think or believe because of the Press  report showing a divided nation over war  powers light caused them to reconsider  actions wherein you found the united  states united and together on the basic  proposition of whether or not we ought  to be in the Persian Gulf lastly on the  general proposition of the extent to  which the United States can commit  itself of course we're still bound by  treaties and obligations some of which  were entered into at the end of World  War two at a time when the United States  enjoyed such a  in position militarily in the world that  there was no question about our ability  to respond to conflicts whether they be  in Europe or in South East Asia or at  any other part of the world today of  course that has dramatically changed and  I think it would be an appropriate and  proper time for this president or the  next president to reassess our ability  to live up and to fulfill the military  obligations included in some of those  treaties and to insist if we're going to  maintain them that there be a far  greater degree of reciprocity in some of  those arrangements I don't think it's  conceivable or possible for the United  States today to be involved in as broad  as wide away militarily is it might like  to think it could I think our allies  must recognize this I begin to believe  our adversaries must be sensitive to it  as well I happen to believe that the  kind of conflict we're going to be faced  with as we complete this century and  into the next one will be the kind of  conflict we're seeing in the Persian  Gulf I would suggest that we may find  ourselves in situations in Central  America or the Caribbean or in other  parts of the world where we're going to  need the ability to respond in a highly  mobile fashion very effectively very  quickly to be able to protect harbors to  be able to protect and remove of armed  forces and individuals and it seems to  me we have paid far too little attention  to those needs over the last several  years with far too concentrated  concentration on our ability to win bay  strategic conflict and I think those of  those gaps were made glaringly apparent  when we discovered for instance that we  lacked the kind of mining or of the  anti-mining of ships that were so needed  in the Persian Gulf only a few weeks ago  those gaps I believe will now be made up  but I believe that if we readjusted ease  on some of those levels then we will be  able to maintain our commitments as we  see more of these brush fires of Percy  but at this particular juncture I would  strongly urge that the president take  advantage of this congressional  opportunity to move forward on the War  Powers whether or not anyone likes the  law whether or not you believe its  constitutional or not is certainly a  legitimate debate but the fact of the  matter is it is the law of the land and  it seems to me when presidents I choose  to ignore those  cause then we of course breed the kind  of content contempt and division within  our society the lack of cohesion which  is absolutely necessary to have a  cohesive and effective foreign policy  and I thank you for listen good  afternoon in looking at the question of  the costs of Defense of any of the  countries from which you come I think we  have to be careful that we don't focus  only on the issue of the dollar costs a  dollar costs and how we divide them  between ourselves and our allies are  really a purely political issue there's  no way you can set a standard either in  accordance with what the United States  is paid in the past in accordance with  percentages of gross national product or  any other kind of yardstick it's a  purely political issue and I would  suggest to you from overseas that the  politics of the United States are such  that the public is becoming weary of the  costs of defending our allies in a joint  defense effort and public is going to  call for a reduction in those costs in  time ahead but what I'd like to do when  picking up where senator Dodd made some  references at the end of his talk is to  point out that there are other costs  that are even more important to the  United States and to our friends and  allies than the dollar costs of Defense  of the Allied territories these are the  costs in terms of the impact on American  military readiness our participer tissy  paiting in joint defense efforts for  instance preparations for the joint  defense of Europe have so dominated  American military thinking over the past  forty two years since World  or to that our military has been less  than ready for other forms of combat the  proof of this lies in the fact that we  have had less than acceptable  performance from our military in almost  every instance of actual combat  employment since World War two we fought  to a tie in Korea we lost in Vietnam we  failed miserably on the deserts of Iran  we won in little Grenada but with  greater loss of life and more confusion  and was warranted now if I sound unduly  critical above the American military  performance let me assure you that it  hurts me to do that because I'm a  military professional than in part  responsible for this but you as people  from the business world are accustomed  to watching the bottom line the bottom  line for any military establishment is  victory on the battlefield and the  United States military has not had a  record since world war two of consistent  victories there are many people to blame  some inside the military and some  without but I think a major contributing  factor has been that our political  leaders and our military men have given  inordinate attention to the defense of  our allies and most particularly our  Western European allies now in preparing  for defense of Western Europe we think  about large-scale combat against a  highly sophisticated and capable enemy  we think in terms of a particular type  of terrain and we think of having the  benefit of pre-positioned troops and  equipment now in this kind of  circumstance for instance large tanks  heavy artillery are perfectly fine even  though they're very difficult to  transport to even more remote areas of  the world and difficult to maneuver in  terrain like jungles now it's no wonder  then I believe with this emphasis on  heavy tanks and artillery and the  analogous forces and the other services  that we stumbled in the jungles of  Vietnam no wonder that we did not have  the training or the right kind of  helicopters to deal with a small-scale  stealthy rescue operation in Iran I  believe the United States today needs to  de-emphasize our role in the defense of  Europe when we talk about doing our  military planning in our military  procurement and we must be ready for the  more likely contingencies in other parts  of the world for instance a tank that is  eighty percent optimum for Europe but  also eighty percent optimum for being  transported to remote areas would be  preferable for us to build instead of  the ones we're building today which can  only fit into our largest transport  aircraft by ones or maybe tues in short  i believe the United States needs and  will be looking for in the years just  ahead a more balanced outlook in our  military planning now a second problem  which arises from our focus on the  defense of Europe South Korea and Japan  is a false dependence on nuclear weapons  42 years ago the United States had a  monopoly on nuclear weapons at that time  it made eminent sense for us to threaten  the devastation of the Soviet homeland  if the Soviets were to invade any one of  our allies it was conveniently a very  cheap form of  defense but when in the 1950s the  Soviets gained a capability to retaliate  with nuclear weapons against the  American homeland it was no longer a  satisfactory strategy for the United  States it became a charade something  that we would not ever really do now  while I hope we would not hesitate to  respond in kind if the Soviets should  attack American soil with nuclear  weapons I do not believe an American  president would launch nuclear weapons  in the defense of Western Europe South  Korea or Japan put yourself in his shoes  whether we launched those weapons from  Omaha or from the weapons we've placed  in Europe and the Far East we would have  to anticipate a response against  American territory if the detonation  were on Soviet soil the response would  be on American soil we would be trading  Washington for Moscow in the defense of  bond or soul or Tokyo for a president  the near certainty of some substantial  nuclear devastation in our own country  whether of Washington or some other city  without way even the loss of Western  Europe to a Soviet invasion the reason  for that is that there is still or there  would be still some hope a recovering  from the loss of Western Europe but  there would be no recovery from the  nuclear devastation of Washington or  other cities and in addition there would  be the danger facing any president who  initiated the use of nuclear weapons  that it would spread into a worldwide  Holocaust and I believe that that kind  of danger would clinch a president's  decision against initiating the use of  nuclear weapons now in the 1960s we  realized that this was the situation  that this nuclear strategy for the  defense of allies was not sound but we  hated to admit we avoided admitting it  for fear that our allies would not  afford the cost of the necessary  conventional forces to deter invasion by  communist forces instead the United  States gradually built up a store of  shorter range nuclear weapons in Western  Europe and in the Far East and we  profess the policy that if there were an  invasion of any of our allies we would  respond with those weapons against those  immediate forces whether they were in  eastern Germany or North Korea or  wherever that was very logical but the  result of this has been an illogical  widely differing interpretation of what  we have termed the American nuclear  umbrella our allies in Europe believe  there's a nuclear umbrella over both  eastern and western Europe and in Korea  and Japan they believe there's a nuclear  umbrella over that and the Allies look  on this umbrella in the following terms  that if inside the umbrella there is a  conventional war and our allies are  losing the United States will launch  nuclear weapons outside that umbrella  over that umbrella to Moscow now they  think too that while there may be some  nuclear weapons coming back but they'll  all land in the United States and the  nuclear destruction will all be outside  this on brother but since the 60s the  American view of the nuclear umbrella is  that if there's a war inside one of  these umbrellas and our allies are  losing will launch these short-range  tactical nuclear weapons all inside the  umbrella and all the nuclear destruction  will be contained therein now the recent  negotiations for an intermediate-range  nuclear weapons treaty with the Soviet  Union have brought all this  inconsistency out into the open our  European  allies for instance can see very clearly  that if we're not willing to launch  weapons at the Soviet Union from the  United States and we then take all of  the shorter nuclear weapons that could  hit the Soviet Union from their  territory and dispense with them there's  only one prospect left and that's this  nuclear battle with short short range  weapons inside the umbrella and that  looks to our allies quite understandably  as though it's self-defense by mutual  nuclear suicide in my view it's only a  matter of time until our allies realize  that this is not a suitable defense  strategy for them and yet the greatest  danger of this myth of nuclear defense  of our allies is that the more we in the  United States talk about this form of  Defense the more likely it is in a  period of tension and crisis that we may  have nuclear war by miscalculation or  mistake and so in sum I would leave you  with a couple of thoughts please the  first is that the United States needs to  move away from its focus of defense on a  defence of its allies and be more  prepared to defend allied and American  interest in other areas of the world as  we are today in the Persian Gulf and  secondly but the increased risks of  nuclear war that come from this myth of  reliance on nuclear weapons to  counterbalance communist conventional  power is just not worth it thank you

