d the united  states united and together on the basic  proposition of whether or not we ought  to be in the Persian Gulf lastly on the  general proposition of the extent to  which the United States can commit  itself of course we're still bound by  treaties and obligations some of which  were entered into at the end of World  War two at a time when the United States  enjoyed such a  in position militarily in the world that  there was no question about our ability  to respond to conflicts whether they be  in Europe or in South East Asia or at  any other part of the world today of  course that has dramatically changed and  I think it would be an appropriate and  proper time for this president or the  next president to reassess our ability  to live up and to fulfill the military  obligations included in some of those  treaties and to insist if we're going to  maintain them that there be a far  greater degree of reciprocity in some of  those arrangements I don't think it's  conceivable or possible for the United  States today to be involved in as broad  as wide away militarily is it might like  to think it could I think our allies  must recognize this I begin to believe  our adversaries must be sensitive to it  as well I happen to believe that the  kind of conflict we're going to be faced  with as we complete this century and  into the next one will be the kind of  conflict we're seeing in the Persian  Gulf I would suggest that we may find  ourselves in situations in Central  America or the Caribbean or in other  parts of the world where we're going to  need the ability to respond in a highly  mobile fashion very effectively very  quickly to be able to protect harbors to  be able to protect and remove of armed  forces and individuals and it seems to  me we have paid far too little attention  to those needs over the last several  years with far too concentrated  concentration on our ability to win bay  strategic conflict and I think those of  those gaps were made glaringly apparent  when we discovered for instance that we  lacked the kind of mining or of the  anti-mining of ships that were so needed  in the Persian Gulf only a few weeks ago  those gaps I believe will now be made up  but I believe that if we readjusted ease  on some of those levels then we will be  able to maintain our commitments as we  see more of these brush fires of Percy  but at this particular juncture I would  strongly urge that the president take  advantage of this congressional  opportunity to move forward on the War  Powers whether or not anyone likes the  law whether or not you believe its  constitutional or not is certainly a  legitimate debate but the fact of the  matter is it is the law of the land and  it seems to me when presidents I choose  to ignore those  cause then we of course breed the kind  of content contempt and division within  our society the lack of cohesion which  is absolutely necessary to have a  cohesive and effective foreign policy  and I thank you for listen good  afternoon in looking at the question of  the costs of Defense of any of the  countries from which you come I think we  have to be careful that we don't focus  only on the issue of the dollar costs a  dollar costs and how we divide them  between ourselves and our allies are  really a purely political issue there's  no way you can set a standard either in  accordance with what the United States  is paid in the past in accordance with  percentages of gross national product or  any other kind of yardstick it's a  purely political issue and I would  suggest to you from overseas that the  politics of the United States are such  that the public is becoming weary of the  costs of defending our allies in a joint  defense effort and public is going to  call for a reduction in those costs in  time ahead but what I'd like to do when  picking up where senator Dodd made some  references at the end of his talk is to  point out that there are other costs  that are even more important to the  United States and to our friends and  allies than the dollar costs of Defense  of the Allied territories these are the  costs in terms of the impact on American  military readiness our participer tissy  paiting in joint defense efforts for  instance preparations for the joint  defense of Europe have so dominated  American military thinking over the past  forty two years since World  or to that our military has been less  than ready for other forms of combat the  proof of this lies in the fact that we  have had less than acceptable  performance from our military in almost  every instance of actual combat  employment since World War two we fought  to a tie in Korea we lost in Vietnam we  failed miserably on the deserts of Iran  we won in little Grenada but with  greater loss of life and more confusion  and was warranted now if I sound unduly  critical above the American military  performance let me assure you that it  hurts me to do that because I'm a  military professional than in part  responsible for this but you as people  from the business world are accustomed  to watching the bottom line the bottom  line for any military establishment is  victory on the battlefield and the  United States military has not had a  record since world war two of consistent  victories there are many people to blame  some inside the military and some  without but I think a major contributing  factor has been that our political  leaders and our military men have given  inordinate attention to the defense of  our allies and most particularly our  Western European allies now in preparing  for defense of Western Europe we think  about large-scale combat against a  highly sophisticated and capable enemy  we think in terms of a particular type  of terrain and we think of having the  benefit of pre-positioned troops and  equipment now in this kind of  circumstance for instance large tanks  heavy artillery are perfectly fine even  though they're very difficult to  transport to even more remote areas of  the world and difficult to maneuver in  terrain like jungles now it's no wonder  then I believe with this emphasis on  heavy tanks and artillery and the  analogous forces and the other services  that we stumbled in the jungles of  Vietnam no wonder that we did not have  the training or the right kind of  helicopters to deal with a small-scale  stealthy rescue operation in Iran I  believe the United States today needs to  de-emphasize our role in the defense of  Europe when we talk about doing our  military planning in our military  procurement and we must be ready for the  more likely contingencies in other parts  of the world for instance a tank that is  eighty percent optimum for Europe but  also eighty percent optimum for being  transported to remote areas would be  preferable for us to build instead of  the ones we're building today which can  only fit into our largest transport  aircraft by ones or maybe tues in short  i believe the United States needs and  will be looking for in the years just  ahead a more balanced outlook in our  military planning now a second problem  which arises from our focus on the  defense of Europe South Korea and Japan  is a false dependence on nuclear weapons  42 years ago the United States had a  monopoly on nuclear weapons at that time  it made eminent sense for us to threaten  the devastation of the Soviet homeland  if the Soviets were to invade any one of  our allies it was conveniently a very  cheap form of  defense but when in the 1950s the  Soviets gained a capability to retaliate  with nuclear weapons against the  American homeland it was no longer a  satisfactory strategy for the United  States it became a charade something  that we would not ever really do now  while I hope we would not hesitate to  respond in kind if the Soviets should  attack American soil with nuclear  weapons I do not believe an American  president would launch nuclear weapons  in the defense of Western Europe South  Korea or Japan put yourself in his shoes  whether we launched those weapons from  Omaha or from the weapons we've placed  in Europe and the Far East we would have  to anticipate a response against  American territory if the detonation  were on Soviet soil the response would  be on American soil we would be trading  Washington for Moscow in the defense of  bond or soul or Tokyo for a president  the near certainty of some substantial  nuclear devastation in our own country  whether of Washington or some other city  without way even the loss of Western  Europe to a Soviet invasion the reason  for that is that there is still or there  would be still some hope a recovering  from the loss of Western Europe but  there would be no recovery from the  nuclear devastation of Washington or  other cities and in addition there would  be the danger facing any president who  initiated the use of nuclear weapons  that it would spread into a worldwide  Holocaust and I believe that that kind  of danger would clinch a president's  decision against initiating the use of  nuclear weapons now in the 1960s we  realized that this was the situation  that this nuclear strategy for the  defense of allies was not sound but we  hated to admit we avoided admitting it  for fear that our allies would not  afford the cost of the necessary  conventional forces to deter invasion by  communist forces instead the United  States gradually built up a store of  shorter range nuclear weapons in Western  Europe and in the Far East and we  profess the policy that if there were an  invasion of any of our allies we would  respond with those weapons against those  immediate forces whether they were in  eastern Germany or North Korea or  wherever that was very logical but the  result of this has been an illogical  widely differing interpretation of what  we have termed the American nuclear  umbrella our allies in Europe believe  there's a nuclear umbrella over both  eastern and western Europe and in Korea  and Japan they believe there's a nuclear  umbrella over that and the Allies look  on this umbrella in the following terms  that if inside the umbrella there is a  conventional war and our allies are  losing the United States will launch  nuclear weapons outside that umbrella  over that umbrella to Moscow now they  think too that while there may be some  nuclear weapons coming back but they'll  all land in the United States and the  nuclear destruction will all be outside  this on brother but since the 60s the  American view of the nuclear umbrella is  that if there's a war inside one of  these umbrellas and our allies are  losing will launch these short-range  tactical nuclear weapons all inside the  umbrella and all the nuclear destruction  will be contained therein now the recent  negotiations for an intermediate-range  nuclear weapons treaty with the Soviet  Union have brought all this  inconsistency out into the open our  European  allies for instance can see very clearly  that if we're not willing to launch  weapons at the Soviet Union from the  United States and we then take all of  the shorter nuclear weapons that could  hit the Soviet Union from their  territory and dispense with them there's  only one prospect left and that's this  nuclear battle with short short range  weapons inside the umbrella and that  looks to our allies quite understandably  as though it's self-defense by mutual  nuclear suicide in my view it's only a  matter of time until our allies realize  that this is not a suitable defense  strategy for them and yet the greatest  danger of this myth of nuclear defense  of our allies is that the more we in the  United States talk about this form of  Defense the more likely it is in a  period of tension and crisis that we may  have nuclear war by miscalculation or  mistake and so in sum I would leave you  with a couple of thoughts please the  first is that the United States needs to  move away from its focus of defense on a  defence of its allies and be more  prepared to defend allied and American  interest in other areas of the world as  we are today in the Persian Gulf and  secondly but the increased risks of  nuclear war that come from this myth of  reliance on nuclear weapons to  counterbalance communist conventional  power is just not worth it thank you
 

